# Line endings, decided here so they stop being decided by whoever last touched a file.
#
# THIS PROJECT IS EDITED FROM TWO OPERATING SYSTEMS AND BUILT ON A THIRD MACHINE. Without this file
# git guesses per checkout, and the guesses disagree: a file added from Windows lands in the index
# with CRLF, a Mac checkout gets LF, and CI gets whichever its runner produces. A test whose regex
# ends in `$` then passes on one and fails on another, which is a real failure this project has
# already paid for once.
#
# LF IN THE INDEX, WHATEVER THE WORKING TREE LOOKS LIKE. Visual Studio, MSBuild and PowerShell are
# all content with LF, so there is nothing to trade away by picking one.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate them and never offers them as a diff.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.wav   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.onnx  binary
*.bin   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.pdb   binary

# EVIDENCE, LEFT BYTE-FOR-BYTE. The workflow rule is to preserve the founder-tested proof and the
# spike results, and normalising them would rewrite files whose whole value is being exactly what was
# run and measured. `-text` means git stores and checks them out unchanged, which also stops this
# change touching a single existing byte anywhere in the repository.
#
# Seven of the spike result files are CRLF in the index today and two files in the proof tree are
# MIXED. All nine stay that way on purpose.
spikes/**                   -text
src/EnviousWispr/**         -text
src/EnviousWispr.Smoke/**   -text

# The macOS snapshot is a copy of another repository's source, kept for parity comparisons. Its
# bytes belong to that repository rather than to this one.
macos-source/**             -text
